course: dictionary
lesson: 15425
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Stakes» can mean wooden posts in the ground, or money risked in a bet. The words around it tell you which meaning the writer means.
explain: «The gardener pushed stakes into the soil to hold up the tomato plants.» Stakes are posts here, because you push posts into soil and tie plants to them.
explain: «The stakes are high, so the team must play its best.» Here stakes means what can be won or lost. Check by swapping in your meaning: «the things at risk are high» still makes sense.
fact: «Stakes» is the plural of «stake», a noun. «Stake» can also be a verb: «She stakes her money on the race.»
fact: A «stake» can be a pointed wooden or metal post pushed into the ground, for example to hold up plants or a tent.
fact: «High stakes» means a lot can be won or lost, so a match or game matters a great deal.
fact: «At stake» means at risk, as in «Our team's place in the final is at stake.»
fact: In history, people were sometimes tied to a wooden post called a stake and burned, as in «burned at the stake».
exercise: «The gardener pushed wooden stakes into the soil to hold up the beans.» What are the stakes here? | A share in a business | Money won in a bet | *Pointed posts pushed into the ground | Small cakes for a picnic
exercise: «The stakes are high in the final match.» What does stakes mean here? | Wooden posts for a tent | *What can be won or lost | Loud cheers from the crowd | Sweet treats for the winners
exercise: «Mum has a stake in the new shop.» What does stake mean? | A loud noise from the street | A tall wooden post | *A share of ownership in the business | Something you eat at lunch
test: Which phrase means that something is at risk? | A high stake in the sky | Up in the air | Stake out the field | *At stake
test: In «He stakes his bike on the game», what kind of word is stakes? | A noun for wooden posts | A plural noun for shares | *A verb meaning risks something | A place to tie a horse
exam: Which sentence uses stakes to mean wooden posts? | «He has a stake in the farm» | *«She hammered the stakes into the ground to hold the tent» | «They stake their pride on winning» | «The stakes are high in this game»
